Animal Trainer:
  An animal trainer would have high dexterity and charisma,
  with the other attributes being on even footing for the
  remaining character points.  Animal trainers, alone of
  all roles, would be able, depending on luck, to tame
  animals just by #chatting with them.  Also, they would
  be able to tame more animals with food than other roles
  can do -- all d except lycanthropes would be tamable 
  with tripe, for example.  They would be restricted in
  most fighting skills but could get expert in whip (and
  would start with one) and would be able to learn 
  two-weapon combat.  I was thinking to maybe also let 
  them get at least basic skill in boomerang, just because 
  most existing roles can't, but that's negotiable.  They
  would start with basic skill in riding and be able to
  reach expert, and alone among all the roles they would
  be able to ride bareback, without a saddle, although
  this would be dependent on luck.  They could also get
  basic skill in enchantment spells.  Starting equipment
  would include gloves, the aforementioned whip, and a
  magic whistle.  The starting pet would be something
  out of the ordinary line, but I haven't decided what
  exactly.  The quest leader would be Jack Hanna.

Linguist:
  Linguists would by similar in principle to archaeologists,
  but without the pick or touchstone.  As compensation,
  they would always (if not stunned/confused/blind/etc) be 
  able to successfully read any non-cursed book.  Their 
  special spell would be identify.  I haven't fully thought 
  through the rest of this role.

Missionary:
  This would be the most challenging to implement.
  All aspects of religion -- including alignment record
  and prayer timeout -- would work very differently for
  this role than for all others.  (Think:  Evangelical
  Christian.)  Everything would be cross-aligned for 
  them (including all three colors of unicorns, all
  altars, all priests), and all of the dieties of the
  pantheon would be considered "idols".  There would be
  hefty penalties for "idolatry", i.e., having anything
  to do with altars or priests or the other aspects
  of the usual NetHack pantheon.  To balance that out,
  they would be able to (and, indeed, would _need_ to)
  #pray much more often; prayer would be significantly
  less effective on a case-by-case basis, but taken in
  total the cumulative effects would be substantial.
  (They would call the diety that they pray to "God".)
  Among other things, there would be a small chance
  with each successful prayer of receiving a point of
  divine protection.

  Starting equipment would include a new item, a book
  of scripture; reading it would decrease the prayer
  timeout, but this would only work for Missionaries.
  One of the major challenges of the role would be
  keeping this item where it is readily accessible
  but not subject to destruction e.g. from fire,
  because it would be difficult to replace and
  more or less impossible to survive without.  I
  am still thinking about how the endgame would 
  work; it would be different.

  Oh, did I mention that it would be murder to kill
  anything represented by @, h, H, or G, regardless
  of whether it's peaceful or hostile?  Yeah.  (I
  *might* be persuaded to make an exception for the
  Wizard of Yendor and/or the high priest of Moloch.
  Possibly.)  This role would be a challenge to play, 
  as well as a challenge to implement.

I was also working on ideas for a couple of custom races.

Rimuthi (masculine singular noun Rimuthid, feminine 
singular noun Rimuthis, neuter singular noun Rimuth,
plural noun Rimuthi, adjective Rimuthi) are a race
with three genders (masculine, feminine, neuter) of
intelligent advanced aliens.  This race is meant to 
be more of a challenge than the stock ones, but it 
does have some advantages.  Their single most
obvious visual characteristic is pale lavendar skin.  
They can achieve high dexterity and intelligence (25 
each) but have low caps on constitution and strength 
(maybe about 14; I'd have to play-test for balance).  

Rimuthi cannot wear helms (because of head shape) or
gloves (because of finger count and length) but can 
wear all other armor.  They are physically incapable 
of digesting raw meat (mainly, non-vegetarian corpses) 
but can eat prepared food (e.g., from tins).  They
have intrinsic slow digestion as a racial trait,
probably their single greatest advantage.  

Like humans, Rimuthi do not have infravision.

They do not, however, have ears.  They communicate 
either telepathically or via sign language.  Thus, 
in order to #chat with a monster, it must either be
telepathic or able to be seen by the Rimuth and
vice versa.  Not all monsters can respond to sign
language.  Many animals can, but the response may 
be perceived differently (e.g., instead of yipping 
a dog may wag its tail; in fact, it's probably
doing both, but a hearing person notices the yip
as the more prominent aspect of the response).
Shopkeepers and aligned priests do know sign 
language, so you can #chat with them if you can
see them and they can see you; also, your hands
must be available (not welded to a cursed weapon)
to #chat with anything that's not telepathic.
The Wizard of Yendor, notably, can be heard even 
without ears; presumably he speaks directly into 
your mind via magic.  Most other humanoid-type 
creatures do not respond to sign language.  Angels 
and demons can use sign language only if they have
hands.  Also, most messages that start with "You hear" 
are simply not printed; a few may show up in altered 
form e.g. if you can feel the vibrations that a 
hearing person would recognize as a rumbling sound.  
The one advantage of not hearing is that it makes 
you immune to attacks that rely on sound, notably 
a cockatrice's hissing.  (Foocubi are irrelevant, 
though; they wouldn't be anatomically compatible 
with Rimuthi physiology in any case.)

Rimuthi are generally neutral.

Rimuthi could be archaeologists, healers, wizards,
or linguists.  I haven't decided whether they 
could be priests or not.  I thought about allowing
them to be tourists, but I think that might be a
bit overpowered, especially in the late game.

